        "B_of32_on_dtype_fp16s_argument": "NEEDS 10, FIXES 10, disjoint from A's and C's rows.  A SEAM crossing, INBOUND, separable by row.",
        "C_no_of32_on_dtype_fp8_tos_answer": "NEEDS 40, FIXES 10 -- and the ONLY site with a row set of its own: those 10 are fp8_decode's EARLY nan/inf returns, which never build the intermediate `v`.  A SEAM crossing, OUTBOUND.",
        "A_of32_inside_fp8_decode": "NEEDS 30, FIXES 0, and NEEDS(A) is a SUBSET of NEEDS(C): NOT separable from C by ANY fixture.  The same error one call INSIDE the lane, on C's path, reachable only through C.  Load-bearing for 30 rows, so not incidental -- but not a seam crossing either, and the 12-row probe could not tell it from C because it carried exactly ONE fp8_to row, which needs both."
      },
      "why_the_conversion_is_forced_ONTO_the_seam": "The alternative spelling -- fp8_decode answers a VALUE and the seam is untouched -- is red on EXACTLY the 10 rows C alone fixes, because fp8_decode returns straight from its nan/inf branches without building `v`, so a conversion placed at its `return` is bypassed by 10 of its 44 reachable rows.  Measured, not assumed: that is why the repair cannot be written one call inward.",
      "locality": "The repair moved 41 of 98 rows and NONE of them a `loc_*` row (bf16, fp8_from -- seams where no VALUE crosses).  Its diff mentions no other ABI's token: a CONTENT fence, because the historical `js-repair-abi4` arm moved five `abi123_*` rows and was ABI-2's repair wearing ABI-4's name."
